-- title:
--   Frobenius twist and cyclic-quotient j at a Tate point
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and for a valuation subring $A$ of a field the notation $\kappa_A$ is used for its residue field.
--
--   **The supersingular frame.** Let $q$ be a prime with $5\le q$, and $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q\nmid M'$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$, i.e. satisfying `A.LiesOverPrime q`: the image of $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; write $\kappa=\kappa_A$. A finite set $W$ of places of the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ — the subfield of $\kappa((t))$ generated over $\kappa$ by the two Laurent series `jqModC` and `jqNModC` at level $M'$ — is given, and the hypothesis `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places for $q$: those places $w$ that are rational, are affine geometric places, and whose value at the geometric generator lies in the supersingular $j$-set `ssJSet q κ`. A place here is a valuation subring of the function field, proper, containing the base field and a principal ideal ring. The hypothesis `hle` asks that the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full modular function field of level $M'$ be contained in the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the $X_H$ function field of level $q^2M'$ for $H$ the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$. Further, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from the former field to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,\kappa\,M'$ — a valuation subring of integers, a surjective residue homomorphism with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility with $A$ and with the residue map of $A$, a scaling clause producing nonzero residues, and a map on places preserving degrees and compatible with divisors — and the hypothesis `hR₀` requires that the residue of $R_0$ on a Laurent series with coefficients in $A$ be the coefficientwise reduction of that series. Finally $s$ is a chosen element of $W$.
--
--   **The arithmetic model.** Let $k_0$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ an element lying in $A$; put $A_0=A\cap k_0$, formally `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` require that $A_0$ be a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, Henselian, with algebraically closed residue field; `hκ` requires every element of $A$ to be congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   **Level data at $q\ell'$.** Let $\ell'$ be a prime with $\ell'\neq q$, $3\le\ell'$ and $\ell'\nmid M'$, let $\xi\in k_0$ be a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity, and let `hιξ` provide a ring homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $k_0\subseteq k_0((t))$ equal (`hK`) to the base change to $k_0$ of the $X_H$ function field of level $(q\ell')^2M'$, $H=$ `levelH (q * ℓ') M'` the kernel of $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell')^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell')^\times$; the field $K$ is an $A_0$-algebra in a way compatible with $k_0$. Let $j\in K$ be the element whose Laurent expansion is the image of `jq` under the coefficient embedding, and $j\neq0$. Write $\mathcal C=$ `chartAlgFin A₀ K j` for the chart algebra, i.e. the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A_0[j]$.
--
--   **Moduli machinery.** The hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM` state that level-$\ell'$ Katz level-$p$ structures and $\Gamma_0(p^k)$-kernel polynomials are carried along variable changes of Weierstrass curves over $A_0$-algebras (the latter via `kernelVariableChangeDeg` at degree `gamma0PowDeg`). Further data: group laws $\mathcal G$ over $A_0$ with `h𝒢` chord–tangent ($\mathcal G$ is computed by a points evaluation on every projective model) and `h𝒢O` origin-identity (the unit section is the origin chart section with $x/y$ and $z/y$ vanishing); a level transport $\mathcal T$ for $\mathcal G$ at $q$ with `h𝒯` asserting that $\mathcal T$ transports the Drinfeld sections $P,Q$ along variable changes and coefficient changes; `hVC` and `hCO` asserting, for every $A_0$-algebra and every variable change, resp. every $A_0$-algebra homomorphism, the existence of a graded homomorphism of projective model rings, compatible with the irrelevant ideals, realising that variable change resp. that coefficient change. Let $\mathcal R=$ `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ' M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, the rigid Weierstrass data whose level object is a triple consisting of a family of $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-kernel polynomials for $p\mid M'$, a level-$\ell'$ structure, and a Drinfeld pair at $q$.
--
--   Let $P_0$ be a fine moduli package (`LevelModuliPackageAbs`) for the moduli datum of $\mathcal R$ over $A_0$: an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ with a universal point `univ` representing the functor. Let $x_0$ be a point of this datum over $K$ whose $j$-invariant has Laurent expansion `jqNModC k₀ (q * ℓ')` (`hx₀`), so $x_0$ is the Tate point at $q\ell'$. Let $\mathrm{cl}$ be an $A_0$-algebra homomorphism $B_0\to\mathcal C$ whose composite with the inclusion $\mathcal C\subseteq K$ is the classifying map of $x_0$ (`hclC`). The hypothesis `hμ` (six clauses, summarised here) provides a variable change $C$ over $k_0((t))$ and a raw representative $r$ of $x_0$ over $K$ such that: the class of $r$ is $x_0$; the entries of $C$ are normalised explicitly, $u$ being determined by an equation relating the two coordinates of the cusp point of `cuspPoint k₀ (q*ℓ')` at the vector $![1,0]$ and the constants $1/6$, with $r=-1/12$, $s=-1/2$, $t=1/24$; the curve of $r$ becomes $C\cdot$`tateBase k₀ (q*ℓ')` over $k_0((t))$; for each prime $p\mid M'$ and each coefficient homomorphism $f$ of $k_0$ into a field carrying a primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta$, the $p$-th kernel polynomial of $r$ becomes the variable-change transform of $\prod_a (X-(\mathrm{toricPoint}\,(q\ell')\,\zeta^a)_1)$ over $1\le a\le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p\nmid a$; the level-$\ell'$ datum of $r$ is the cusp datum at the vectors $![q,0]$, $![0,-q]$ transformed by $C$; and there are elements of $K$ realising the coordinates of the cusp datum at $![\ell',0]$, $![0,-\ell']$ transformed by $C$, through which the Drinfeld sections $P$ and $Q$ of $r$ pass. Lastly $\theta:\kappa_{A_0}\to\kappa$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with the residue maps of $A_0$ and $A$ (`hθ`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses the following holds. Let $y$ be a maximal ideal of $\mathcal C$ and $\psi:\mathcal C\to\kappa_{A_0}$ an $A_0$-algebra homomorphism whose vanishing locus is exactly $y$. Let $x$ be a raw object of $\mathcal R$ over $\kappa_{A_0}$ whose class is the image of the universal point under $\psi\circ\mathrm{cl}$, such that the curve of $x$ has no nonzero $q$-torsion point, and let $g$ be a point of that curve together with `hg`: $g$ has exact order $M'$, and for every prime $p\mid M'$ and every $n$, if $n\cdot g$ is an affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ of exact order $p^{v_p(M')}$ then $x_1$ is a root of the $p$-th kernel polynomial of $x$.
--
--   Let $\Omega$ be a field which is an algebra over $\kappa$, $V$ a place of $\Omega$ over $\kappa$, $\mathrm{res}$ a ring homomorphism from the residue field of $\mathcal O_V$ to $\kappa$, and $\rho:\mathcal C\to\mathcal O_V$ a ring homomorphism, subject to the admissibility clauses: $\mathrm{res}$ retracts the structural map of $\kappa$; $\rho$ sends scalars from $A_0$ to $\theta$ of their residues, read in $\Omega$; $\mathrm{res}\circ(\text{residue})\circ\rho=\theta\circ\psi$; $\rho(j)$ is transcendental over $\kappa$; and $\Omega$ is finite over $\kappa(\rho(j))$. Let $\mathcal O_V$ carry an $A_0$-algebra structure, let $\rho_A:B_0\to\mathcal O_V$ be an $A_0$-algebra homomorphism equal to $\rho\circ\mathrm{cl}$, let $x_V$ be a raw object of $\mathcal R$ over $\mathcal O_V$ whose class is the image of the universal point under $\rho_A$, and let $M'\neq0$ in $\Omega$.
--
--   The connectedness clause: let $\chi_Q$ be a ring homomorphism from the origin chart ring of the curve of the Drinfeld pair of $x_V$ to $\Omega$ such that the $\Omega$-point obtained from the section $Q$ by base change along $\mathcal O_V\to\Omega$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}(\chi_Q)$ followed by the origin chart immersion, with $\chi_Q(x/y)=0$ and $\chi_Q(z/y)=0$; that is, the generic Drinfeld point $Q$ is the origin.
--
--   The level-symmetry clause: let $\gamma\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ lie in $\Gamma(\ell')$ and in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and have both diagonal entries $\equiv0\bmod q$, let $\tau$ be a $k_0$-algebra automorphism of $K$ with `IsLevelAutAt k₀ (q*ℓ') ξ (q*ℓ') ((q*ℓ')^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ') M') γ⁻¹ K τ`, i.e. for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f,g$ on $\Gamma_H$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ and $p_g$ with nonzero series, all $x\in K$ whose expansion is the ratio of those series, and every embedding $\iota$ of $k_0$ into $\mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$, the coefficientwise image under $\iota$ of the expansion of $\tau x$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$. Assume `hpres` that $\tau$ preserves $\mathcal C$, and `hTR` that for every $h\in\mathcal C$ all of whose Laurent coefficients of $\tau h$ lie in the maximal ideal of $A_0$ one has $\rho(h)=0$ in $\Omega$.
--
--   Then there exist a field $\Omega_1$ which is an algebra over $\kappa$, a place $V_1$ of $\Omega_1$ over $\kappa$, a homomorphism $\mathrm{res}_1$ from the residue field of $\mathcal O_{V_1}$ to $\kappa$, and a ring homomorphism $\rho_1:\mathcal C\to\mathcal O_{V_1}$, such that the same five admissibility clauses hold: $\mathrm{res}_1$ retracts the structural map of $\kappa$; $\rho_1$ sends scalars from $A_0$ to $\theta$ of their residues read in $\Omega_1$; $\mathrm{res}_1\circ(\text{residue})\circ\rho_1=\theta\circ\psi$; $\rho_1(j)$ is transcendental over $\kappa$; and $\Omega_1$ is finite over $\kappa(\rho_1(j))$. Moreover there exist a Weierstrass curve $\mathcal E$ over $\mathcal O_{V_1}$ whose reduction has nonzero discriminant, and a point $\mathrm{gen}$ of $\mathcal E$ over $\Omega_1$ of exact order $M'$, such that:
--
--   (i) the reduction $\mathrm{reduceHom}\,\mathrm{gen}$ again has exact order $M'$, and the image under $\mathrm{res}_1$ of the class of the pair (reduced curve, reduced generator) in the $\Gamma_0(M')$-moduli set at level $M'$ equals the image of the class of the pair $(x.\mathrm{curve},g)$ under $(\mathrm{frobenius}\,\kappa\,q)\circ\theta$, the residue field $\kappa$ having characteristic $q$ because $q$ is a non-unit of $A$;
--
--   (ii) for every algebraically closed field $\Omega'$ which is an $\Omega_1$-algebra, every nonzero $d\mid M'$, and the element `qExpand k₀ ℓ' (coeffEmb k₀ (qExpand ℚ d jq))` of $k_0((t))$ assumed to lie in $K$ and in $\mathcal C$, the cyclic-quotient $j$-invariant $c_4^3/\Delta$ of the quotient of $\mathcal E$ over $\Omega'$ by the subgroup generated by the image of $(M'/d)\cdot\mathrm{gen}$, taken at level $d$, equals the image in $\Omega'$ of $\rho_1$ applied to that element of $\mathcal C$.
--
--   This is the connected-branch half of the Tate-divisor (quotient-model) step in the construction of a supersingular specialisation of the moduli of elliptic curves with $\Gamma_0(M')$, level-$\ell'$ and Drinfeld level-$q$ structure: along a branch on which the generic Drinfeld point is the origin, the reduction of the curve read off by $\rho$ is identified with the Frobenius twist of the supersingular point $(x.\mathrm{curve},g)$, and the cyclic quotients of that curve are shown to compute the branch readings of the $j$-expansions at $q^{d\ell'}$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_isModuliPlaceOf_map_frobenius_of_forall_evalAt_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_isModuliPlaceOf_map_frobenius_of_forall_evalAt_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint).
